we make hasharray for 26 alphabets character having size=26 then is this correct hash[s[j]]=hash[s[j]-'a']

Short answer: Nope, that’s not correct 🙂
If your hash array size is 26, you must use s[j] - 'a'.

Why?

You made something like this:

vector<int> hash(26, 0);


This means valid indices are:

0 to 25


But s[j] is a character, like:

'a' → ASCII 97
'b' → ASCII 98
...


So if you do:

hash[s[j]]


you’re trying to access hash[97], hash[98] 😬
→ out of bounds error

✅ Correct way

Convert character to index using 'a':

hash[s[j] - 'a']++;


Because:

'a' - 'a' = 0
'b' - 'a' = 1
...
'z' - 'a' = 25


Perfect fit for size 26 ✅

When is hash[s[j]] valid?

Only when your hash array size is 256 (for all ASCII characters):

vector<int> hash(256, 0);
hash[s[j]]++;

Quick rule to remember 🔑

26 size → hash[ch - 'a'] (only lowercase a–z)

256 size → hash[ch] (any ASCII character)

